#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133653 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133838 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40bbc65e-5ee4-5ce6-aaf7-d0f9968c0c51" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"}] :question/id #uuid "792b0c63-15eb-5b0e-b5d6-01ae5b68eeca" :question/math-academy-id "e-7134" :question/problem "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ and $y=1-x$ from $y=0$ to $y=1,$ as shown below, then what is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x^2y+xy^2) \\, \\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?\n\n![](images/0e/0e4cf57408f9ca18dc4613317b8b9bc8b3149da0bf273ad03a8a8a48a6e6f454.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ and $y=1-x$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\sqrt{x-2} & \\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}+2 \\\\ y=1-x & \\,⟹\\,x=1-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/f0/f0e531c612c84aa0260b318aa1fbe21c9089270b8c4a1079e08583b7fc5a5d90.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\bigg\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad 1-y \\leq x  \\leq y^2+2 \\bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-y}^{{y}^{2}+2}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133654 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133839 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ccbe70d-24f4-5e9c-8536-9ccab99f571b" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"}] :question/id #uuid "e005ebb9-6c14-529e-be8f-9b837e45eef1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7135" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=3-2x$ below the line $y=3.$ What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} f\\left( {x,y} \\right) \\, \\mathrm{d}A\\:$ expressed as a repeated integral?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/ff/ffbe0db1ae1bb082e63b0d97324b43dd78334dfbd1e88aaa0744be039dd0efc6.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=x^2$ and $y=3-2x$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y=3-2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{3-y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{3-y}{2} \\\\ 4y & =(3-y)^{2} \\\\ 4y & =9-6y+{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-10y+9 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies below the line $y=3,$ the $y$-limits are \n\n$$\n1 \\le y \\le 3.\n$$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/3c/3c56798a33ee2da6d928174b67546945be4bf3594d15e89f3ca39119dd4e2c62.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 3, \\quad \\dfrac{3-y}{2} \\leq x  \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{(3-y)/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133655 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdce40e5-8ffe-52cb-8a8e-ac8eaea982f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/id #uuid "ebd5bc16-2df2-5014-a1a2-bdebf2afa195" :question/math-academy-id "e-7136" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{\\sqrt{\\pi}}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\int_0^{y^2} \\frac{\\sin y^2}{y} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\,\\mathrm{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}[x]_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}({y}^{2}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}y\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}]}_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133656 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f7718f-e014-5232-acb1-6773ab79f61d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/id #uuid "ad2ef01a-d49d-5b17-9066-d5c3970caf31" :question/math-academy-id "e-7137" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $x^2+y^2=4$ and $x=0,$ and above the line $y=-1,$ as shown in the picture.\n\n![](images/f9/f93aeee59ad97fde0efceff342e32a19062187261abec865616bfa963f1084a6.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x \\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} \\\\ x=0 & \\,⟹\\,x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=-1,$ the $y$-limits are \n$-1 \\le y \\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/8d/8d77e3caa18191710e056b72ea54e5820e6244d9e90719222deed409d3992c5b.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq x  \\leq \\sqrt{4-y^2} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A = \\int_{-1}^2 \\int_0^{\\sqrt{4-y^2}}\\, xy^2\\, \\mathrm dx\\,   \\mathrm{d}  y\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,x{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}(4-{y}^{2}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}(2{y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(2{y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2})\\,dy & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{10}]}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{32}{10})-(-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133838 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40bbc65e-5ee4-5ce6-aaf7-d0f9968c0c51" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "389c89b0-6f78-515d-b02c-c50614aa0a47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{(y-1)^{2}+2}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380987} {:answer/id #uuid "26a55a09-a269-513d-9fef-14a7053e3105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{(y-1)^{2}+2}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380988} {:answer/id #uuid "3e5b81eb-e01f-5254-a2ec-460fb6a2508a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{(y-1)^{2}+2}^{1}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380989} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a9a48e-3d2f-5df9-87f7-3d40b452ff4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{(y-1)^{2}+2}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380990} {:answer/id #uuid "ee636de4-c87d-5565-b132-6c0707e44a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{(y-1)^{2}+2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26a55a09-a269-513d-9fef-14a7053e3105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{(y-1)^{2}+2}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380988} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd9ae505-6caf-5d53-988d-3ff8f5ae28d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c9859c1-e939-5417-af18-fab39253454a" :question/math-academy-id "q-132384" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2aaf1935bfd8e079782249df3e230ceb40719ac48d0f5d36dd5e1419499771a7.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x=1,$ $y=0$ and $y=2,$ and the curve $x=(y-1)^{2}+2,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}xy\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/72/72cf3a4044e83e63e579b00d3ca246071787ed4d6b44e4e22fcf963dc10917b4.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,1\\le x\\le (y-1)^{2}+2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{(y-1)^{2}+2}xy\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{(y-1)^{2}+2}xy\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133838 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40bbc65e-5ee4-5ce6-aaf7-d0f9968c0c51" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00ed1d86-32de-5d67-aa19-93461d9e2481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{3-y}^{{y}^{3}/8}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475445} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5249f3-ca45-56a6-b574-91443e6bc86e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{3}/8}^{3-y}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475499} {:answer/id #uuid "83f59ee4-8854-554e-8d45-50a43a2ba821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{y}^{3}/8}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-y}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475639} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2c7f9a-ab99-52de-9af7-323fe893b09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3-y}[{\\int }_{{y}^{3}/8}^{2}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475723} {:answer/id #uuid "e2729abb-d481-536b-a55b-43bf63e88602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{y}^{3}/8}^{3-y}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a5249f3-ca45-56a6-b574-91443e6bc86e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{3}/8}^{3-y}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475499} :answer-field/id #uuid "744f55b6-6eed-5e1b-b5e4-d4fa4c34046b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c16e9cb6-870f-5672-9efc-e4072f4b672a" :question/math-academy-id "q-132388" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9c684a6eaf9961b441cb559656aa2689ed0f25f61add19094df16e617ece8daf.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3},\\,$ $x=3-y,$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/2d/2dcc8a77fea8a9fd3e95022edcd86e0a040c54fd30d88a7931c0542fdeaefd0b.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3}\\le x\\le 3-y\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}/8}^{3-y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{3}/8}^{3-y}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133838 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40bbc65e-5ee4-5ce6-aaf7-d0f9968c0c51" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ae2b3d3-a690-5265-999b-62fe58e2e8d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}-4}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266372} {:answer/id #uuid "76c8dc32-3e34-5c4c-b179-012967bb52ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267187} {:answer/id #uuid "9f400707-cba5-52f2-a92a-821eb3b734c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267581} {:answer/id #uuid "bb610374-93f8-567c-8f78-b2bab47a0d42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267824} {:answer/id #uuid "c5084266-9818-5832-bb26-0431cc54fab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{2}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb610374-93f8-567c-8f78-b2bab47a0d42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267824} :answer-field/id #uuid "4319b2c9-45c9-5863-9100-5fb4eaf5d77a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3cfac5a9-6edd-563f-8792-0eaf27928ce8" :question/math-academy-id "q-132402" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bfa591e51f42e8e52b67e61c23b02ea69b03bdecc1c4b815370ce6b2b0b5f213.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the $x$-axis and the curves $x={y}^{2}-4$ and $x=4-{y}^{2},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/85/85d3faac9a27353a8ea5151b3a32f1065aecb02fd07e0b20727c35de0a7fb216.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,{y}^{2}-4\\le x\\le 4-{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdce40e5-8ffe-52cb-8a8e-ac8eaea982f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26a7dd9b-e017-5f3a-8ac2-116d68520887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "343" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381001} {:answer/id #uuid "6842fe58-1814-5da0-b2b5-e8aa3f1ea7d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "207" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381002} {:answer/id #uuid "357c1cd9-e42d-5c09-8c34-8eb3c6795c39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381003} {:answer/id #uuid "34c8f94b-393d-56a8-88a1-afd8c3474d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381004} {:answer/id #uuid "64ec3eb1-c941-574f-b566-b7540e15ac84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "243" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64ec3eb1-c941-574f-b566-b7540e15ac84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "243" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381005} :answer-field/id #uuid "59883c48-3e71-5cdb-8cbd-e6b235b24ad4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f81302b-c373-529b-a5a0-1251b4805ba1" :question/math-academy-id "q-132414" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{3y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{3y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{1}{3}(3y)^{3}+(3y){y}^{2})dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(9{y}^{3}+3{y}^{3})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}12{y}^{3}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}12{y}^{3}\\,dy & ={[3{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =3(3)^{4} \\\\  & =3(81) \\\\  & =243\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdce40e5-8ffe-52cb-8a8e-ac8eaea982f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "277a4541-6f08-5e3e-838b-f01d1516b21c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475492} {:answer/id #uuid "95a06dc1-9e17-565e-a31f-ef0e8161e3db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475664} {:answer/id #uuid "a50ad013-8b48-5e43-a2a6-1c682d4792ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475685} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c25026-72a4-58f2-a297-df0a533795e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475711} {:answer/id #uuid "e9be5c54-8a82-5427-b59e-3743cfd14a94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9be5c54-8a82-5427-b59e-3743cfd14a94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475799} :answer-field/id #uuid "91ff3f61-f524-514d-8f8d-a3656c78181d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a83ee34-1dbf-5891-a221-8c81f41c4ea4" :question/math-academy-id "q-132416" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}y{e}^{x}\\,dx\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}y{e}^{x}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}y{e}^{x}\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[y{e}^{x}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(y{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}-y{e}^{0})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({y}^{2}-y)\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}({y}^{2}-y)\\,dy & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{3}-2)-(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdce40e5-8ffe-52cb-8a8e-ac8eaea982f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b623f36-eb68-544e-a7e5-05d9acdf0e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381015} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b2f28b-4079-5eb5-984a-4af170512514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381016} {:answer/id #uuid "9504c561-f825-5c73-ab9f-071939988f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381017} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5e9db7-bd7d-592b-a932-d8249d8253cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381018} {:answer/id #uuid "ce36cb26-a73b-51ff-8442-c8e214bc0969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9504c561-f825-5c73-ab9f-071939988f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381017} :answer-field/id #uuid "36c24fc7-d1a1-5f71-a6ea-af647a4e2a84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86450b76-caa8-5dc8-9785-42134807827a" :question/math-academy-id "q-132417" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{2y}^{y+1}2xy\\,dx\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{2y}^{y+1}2xy\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}y[{\\int }_{2y}^{y+1}2x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}y[\\,{x}^{2}\\,]_{2y}^{y+1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}y((y+1)^{2}-(2y)^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}y({y}^{2}+2y+1-4{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-3{y}^{3}+2{y}^{2}+y)\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-3{y}^{3}+2{y}^{2}+y)\\,dy & ={[-\\frac{3}{4}{y}^{4}+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{11}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133839 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ccbe70d-24f4-5e9c-8536-9ccab99f571b" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81410832-b6b3-5770-a5c8-ab39c78cd75a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}/4+1}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-2}^{2}(x-{y}^{2})\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381022} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8f5140-7e4d-5c07-9a35-b2c5c8c58b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}/4+1}^{{y}^{2}-2}(x-{y}^{2})\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381023} {:answer/id #uuid "4a5c0904-845d-5506-b9ed-cbbf441c4201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-2}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}/4+1}(x-{y}^{2})\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381024} {:answer/id #uuid "0abdda0e-9501-5f4b-a759-4d62540e6335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-2}^{{y}^{2}/4+1}(x-{y}^{2})\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381025} {:answer/id #uuid "3eec9544-9a54-5558-87b3-c7be335e7ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-2}^{{y}^{2}/4+1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-{y}^{2})\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0abdda0e-9501-5f4b-a759-4d62540e6335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-2}^{{y}^{2}/4+1}(x-{y}^{2})\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381025} :answer-field/id #uuid "944e9043-a2b2-5890-9087-b38a43ff9304" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "808843b5-4ec6-51d5-9767-8d30c84390fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-132497" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9fb82d3ef2fa47cc67e04ae7c43bb2f0fe021adb73063107f9ad16e9627cc451.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed between the curves $x=\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}+1$ and $x={y}^{2}-2,$ above the $x$-axis, as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}(x-{y}^{2})\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}+1 & ={y}^{2}-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}{y}^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)(y+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the $x$-axis, the $y$-limits are\n$0\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/9e/9ed5a24f07ad025cbf47d664ac2c9e2f6525e43b0bc73c0b25eb7e3e5c60ac47.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,{y}^{2}-2\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}+1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-{y}^{2})\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-2}^{{y}^{2}/4+1}(x-{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-2}^{{y}^{2}/4+1}(x-{y}^{2})\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133839 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ccbe70d-24f4-5e9c-8536-9ccab99f571b" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e34dcd5c-c548-555f-9d01-08b2082ee6b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{-{y}^{2}}{\\int }_{2y-3}^{1}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381029} {:answer/id #uuid "e18f17c5-7c1e-57f7-8c6c-914f7ad84ff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y-3}^{-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381030} {:answer/id #uuid "92f50e5a-02c5-577c-aef6-8553b78e20a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-{y}^{2}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y-3}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381031} {:answer/id #uuid "02529dfd-091f-5dfe-8f9c-f37ba8ceaee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2y-3}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381032} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb3ceb7-f1b3-5f08-8d14-04c619324de8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2y-3}^{-{y}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e18f17c5-7c1e-57f7-8c6c-914f7ad84ff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y-3}^{-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381030} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec70df83-3cc5-54d5-84b9-e21b1cbe078e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c102617-2635-5783-b418-6ddb6be7b181" :question/math-academy-id "q-132516" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curve $y=\\sqrt{-x}$ and the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}(x+3),$ above the $x$-axis. What is ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA\\,$ expressed as a repeated integral?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/c4/c4531d05ef2634d04e590b099ba5ec0269c9c00f12dce82442043cad4377db1a.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=\\sqrt{-x}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}(x+3)$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\sqrt{-x} & \\,⟹\\,x=-{y}^{2} \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{2}(x+3) & \\,⟹\\,x=2y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2} & =2y-3 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y-3 & =0 \\\\ (y+3)(y-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the $x$-axis, the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/31/3187c1520326caf6d009cacaf8b13bfc58a3e0eadbbdfd7aab01fb0d4154f476.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,2y-3\\le x\\le -{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y-3}^{-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f7718f-e014-5232-acb1-6773ab79f61d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "098e1e2f-2c04-5779-ad75-dd534e4f624a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266230} {:answer/id #uuid "5477532d-e192-5fac-9b52-f3bd5883216b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266902} {:answer/id #uuid "61c42a32-8f4f-5ec9-8fdd-1ee40a31fe7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267007} {:answer/id #uuid "647b2c0c-92e1-58b6-9dd0-270ced1b1e9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267032} {:answer/id #uuid "7958ef49-c9d9-5771-b972-b067007a8140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5477532d-e192-5fac-9b52-f3bd5883216b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266902} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d60afa9-e4b7-51f3-8fee-bda29b300a3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2da71a28-356e-5389-9334-828f80b33c46" :question/math-academy-id "q-132531" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,dA,$ where $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{6}-0)dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{5}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{5}\\,dy & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{5}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{6}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{32}{3}-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{21}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f7718f-e014-5232-acb1-6773ab79f61d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "561c97d2-3393-5c66-a355-2e331ac87f03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475568} {:answer/id #uuid "770dffba-b473-54d0-8c91-375e002f00da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475614} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac6117c-6ac7-5259-8419-7056da507f1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475620} {:answer/id #uuid "91dabac6-48a9-50c8-9bb2-f954d8bd6dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475659} {:answer/id #uuid "b0edc4cf-07e5-5251-add3-83a790ba9c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ac6117c-6ac7-5259-8419-7056da507f1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475620} :answer-field/id #uuid "9057d0d2-ba1b-5966-80e3-02c53e2d8441" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9c36b7d-47cc-5c7c-bf98-1c203381319d" :question/math-academy-id "q-132532" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}2y{e}^{x}\\,dA,$ where $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2y{e}^{x}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}2y{e}^{x}\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}2y{e}^{x}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2y[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}{e}^{x}\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2y[\\,{e}^{x}\\,]_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2y({e}^{{y}^{2}}-{e}^{0})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2y{e}^{{y}^{2}}-2y)\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2y{e}^{{y}^{2}}-2y)\\,dy & ={[{e}^{{y}^{2}}-{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =({e}^{1}-1)-({e}^{0}-0) \\\\  & =e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133839 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ccbe70d-24f4-5e9c-8536-9ccab99f571b" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28685095-749a-5991-9be2-402bf87a4783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{y/2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475495} {:answer/id #uuid "b50cbc81-f2c6-5823-bd95-abc058fa081b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475713} {:answer/id #uuid "c020b011-abec-5f74-a0d6-2e493cdf3a8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{2}^{y/2}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475735} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6d84dc-f6bf-5a95-aef6-2d114a0e442f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{y/2}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475751} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4f557a-0393-5b7c-9aa1-df8febbf44fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc4f557a-0393-5b7c-9aa1-df8febbf44fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475776} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cda2ff4-5dd7-546b-a1e2-111a796413b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "118a9fcd-c8c0-57b4-8e3a-7c92ff45ac8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-40678" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}$ above the line $y=2.$ What is ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA\\,$ expressed as a repeated integral?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/4e/4e58d7969f0b31206ae8c3385a857c0ad6cf8a42ee79909d51c3a6b58a090852.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ y={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y & =0 \\\\ y(y-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=2,$ the $y$-limits are \n$2\\le y\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/16/16944d6651297f1c13c39ddf3d9160d721f5f29bfa6b0acac0db46c88295964e.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133838 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40bbc65e-5ee4-5ce6-aaf7-d0f9968c0c51" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd89acc-38db-57cd-a1f8-4c035d4d2152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{4-y}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{(16-{y}^{2})/4}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475462} {:answer/id #uuid "85e43e9a-8f5e-52ce-8d25-65c7198df5c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{(16-{y}^{2})/4}^{4-y}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475645} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e735a3-6961-591b-af1d-548b88828135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{4-y}^{(16-{y}^{2})/4}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475737} {:answer/id #uuid "ce17ec5c-4d49-56a6-b9e5-5a66a85b4bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4-y}[{\\int }_{4}^{(16-{y}^{2})/4}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475750} {:answer/id #uuid "d27c3e19-30a2-58ae-b871-a1681a570303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{(16-{y}^{2})/4}[{\\int }_{4-y}^{4}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0e735a3-6961-591b-af1d-548b88828135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{4-y}^{(16-{y}^{2})/4}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475737} :answer-field/id #uuid "35eca8e7-8a6a-55fb-8c74-791eef5e403a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9802711d-167f-5fa9-b90b-5993685135b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-81235" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c94c22139b5efb671b999a08b926eb7c25672eab6fcbefec886ce8523a82788f.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{1}{4}(16-{y}^{2}),\\,$ $x=4-y,$ and the line $y=1,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/e1/e18c6c41d8fb3ffd15bd31741c849094055dbe3090c524e7645ba6463672d23d.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,4-y\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{4}(16-{y}^{2})\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{4-y}^{(16-{y}^{2})/4}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{4-y}^{(16-{y}^{2})/4}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133838 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40bbc65e-5ee4-5ce6-aaf7-d0f9968c0c51" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3764dc81-502d-5d2a-8042-8a86d6318b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{{y}^{2}+2}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348945} {:answer/id #uuid "9e50804d-ec91-55f7-90b7-9cf01b421616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}+2}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349859} {:answer/id #uuid "c899f45b-b31b-5b26-9f8b-ec96072b5a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{2}+2}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350212} {:answer/id #uuid "d4cd1089-805c-512e-8760-571929fec661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}+2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350312} {:answer/id #uuid "f6eb4193-e1b9-5c75-aafc-26f48c07ac19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}+2}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3764dc81-502d-5d2a-8042-8a86d6318b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{{y}^{2}+2}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348945} :answer-field/id #uuid "e498f2cb-485a-533b-8cc9-3a693e233bae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be18145a-4015-5280-9fff-16f18aff3c3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81260" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ What is ${∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y=\\sqrt{x-2} & \\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/bb/bb3249a9580e754c4ef9c33239c2a51a9b57ca9032a6acbeef7bbc532b2d4345.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\le x\\le {y}^{2}+2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{{y}^{2}+2}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351283 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133839 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ccbe70d-24f4-5e9c-8536-9ccab99f571b" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16a22695-2e40-5ff1-b734-4b865d80d56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{2}{\\int }_{1/y}^{2y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348636} {:answer/id #uuid "4c22a166-1f38-5900-a7e1-3b5a6037c01f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{1/y}{\\int }_{2}^{2y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349143} {:answer/id #uuid "873377de-542a-57c6-80ad-a4d571b9b2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1/y}^{2y}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{2}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349677} {:answer/id #uuid "c8991ec1-2d7c-5e83-a094-5776ddcc8682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{2}{\\int }_{2y}^{1/y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350211} {:answer/id #uuid "e57e1146-e6ab-5fb1-93bd-e8da29560915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1/y}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{2y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16a22695-2e40-5ff1-b734-4b865d80d56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{2}{\\int }_{1/y}^{2y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348636} :answer-field/id #uuid "7218ff00-0ee2-5fcd-9641-28a9714d94fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "acecd55c-92a5-5c62-a1ac-e63916806f3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81291" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ where $x>0,$ and $y=\\frac{x}{2},$ below the line $y=2.$ What is ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA\\,$ expressed as a repeated integral?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/22/2209e57f8cbe84ccf2f930c5cd3004ff5009dfb72f3e4b3262517cf9b8f2b119.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $y=\\frac{x}{2}$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ y=\\frac{x}{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y} & =2y \\\\ 2y-\\frac{1}{y} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2{y}^{2}-1}{y} & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies in the first quadrant below the line $y=2$ the $y$-limits are \n$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/1b/1b03741537734087836c22dbb40e2e188825bade657cff129374efe1596bcb3c.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\frac{1}{y}\\le x\\le 2y\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{2}{\\int }_{1/y}^{2y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133839 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ccbe70d-24f4-5e9c-8536-9ccab99f571b" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f195090-3d93-59e1-afd9-0a582f1e9403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{8-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407898} {:answer/id #uuid "21e012b2-7e2a-55b3-9d7c-1a341837e391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{8-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407899} {:answer/id #uuid "2bdf210e-55ed-5de9-80fd-b8bc3c6300fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{8-{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407900} {:answer/id #uuid "f8af2e6f-7f53-52a5-b469-925f72fac328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{8-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407914} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c41421-3be2-50e8-9e6b-0a900263854c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{2}^{8-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f195090-3d93-59e1-afd9-0a582f1e9403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{8-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407898} :answer-field/id #uuid "90293907-fe9e-585f-8155-9888c4d916c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60195b0c-70fb-5843-93ba-a36d5b0f7537" :question/math-academy-id "q-81292" :question/problem "![](images/df/df3acf049579ec4de948d84d04d4fe3012c2232b9699d0471857227f52c78107.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $x={y}^{2},\\,$ $x=8-{y}^{2},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-{y}^{2} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =2{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/27/2722224678aeb1f9ea64f42de2dc4fbf6e139350c291de16500957575fd23850.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 8-{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{8-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{8-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdce40e5-8ffe-52cb-8a8e-ac8eaea982f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48a32f45-bafb-5c3b-a0e0-199cdc707dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349108} {:answer/id #uuid "56fe4a2f-8be4-528c-bfa8-de5d96d975da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349245} {:answer/id #uuid "782f575e-4140-58d0-a03f-8b5c5c85c7f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349553} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe97764-b087-5f82-acf5-badc73c8292f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350277} {:answer/id #uuid "e9acc333-a2c4-54ab-aac3-b3f14d072096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56fe4a2f-8be4-528c-bfa8-de5d96d975da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349245} :answer-field/id #uuid "80b343ea-0943-5bf0-b6d2-b14961b4e1a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c88bbbaa-afbc-50f2-9c9a-541dff759c54" :question/math-academy-id "q-81309" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-1}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-1}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{y-1}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]_{y-1}^{0}\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-1))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-1)-1)\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-1)-1)\\,dy & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-1)-y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-1)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1)-0) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdce40e5-8ffe-52cb-8a8e-ac8eaea982f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26bbc4db-b814-52bd-b3ff-a786602bad48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348779} {:answer/id #uuid "26bde5b4-36d6-575c-9652-f9261a189321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348780} {:answer/id #uuid "294ee22b-5415-5f7c-87fb-cf75ba45c624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348805} {:answer/id #uuid "2962217e-731b-56f6-b321-15ce00f3d9e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348807} {:answer/id #uuid "4acd7113-2239-595d-9214-c7d08e0c617c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2962217e-731b-56f6-b321-15ce00f3d9e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348807} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9bcc58f-cf39-5a29-98cc-d059c827153d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80f565d3-72fd-5b66-9620-41422767e4db" :question/math-academy-id "q-81320" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{3}}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{3}}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{3}}dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}[x]_{1}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}({y}^{3}-1)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(y-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(y-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\,dy & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{y}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(8+\\frac{1}{4})-(\\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f7718f-e014-5232-acb1-6773ab79f61d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "564a011e-7d84-5afe-b400-660c22c62607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382492} {:answer/id #uuid "9eeb7da5-02bc-53cd-8e9b-696b1ca81db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382493} {:answer/id #uuid "c88a38f3-dfb9-5885-a772-66802526c4bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382494} {:answer/id #uuid "d4831a22-e864-59f2-ae77-cad6d38daf40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382495} {:answer/id #uuid "066b28e3-9cf3-5ff6-8a91-a6102f1fe87b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-2e+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "066b28e3-9cf3-5ff6-8a91-a6102f1fe87b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-2e+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382496} :answer-field/id #uuid "c47442ae-5527-5caf-9a94-06d41bf2c9e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e5ab0ab-75de-56ed-a439-c354130b3e57" :question/math-academy-id "q-81381" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}y{e}^{x}\\,dA,$ where $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 2-{y}^{2}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 2-{y}^{2}\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y{e}^{x}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}(y\\cdot {e}^{x})\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}(y\\cdot {e}^{x})\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}{e}^{x}\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[{e}^{x}]_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y({e}^{2-{y}^{2}}-{e}^{{y}^{2}})dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y{e}^{2-{y}^{2}}-y{e}^{{y}^{2}})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y{e}^{2-{y}^{2}}-y{e}^{{y}^{2}})\\,dy & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2-{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{y}^{2}}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{1}+{e}^{1}-({e}^{2}+{e}^{0})) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-2e+1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f7718f-e014-5232-acb1-6773ab79f61d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "407ad84e-74ca-5fc0-921f-8337eef03b66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407902} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8add82-176b-5127-abc9-008b7fd4cd65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407907} {:answer/id #uuid "d2256d50-cc9a-51f4-9bdc-9c4c49897e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407911} {:answer/id #uuid "d506fb6b-f38e-52d5-99c9-4337a51aff70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407912} {:answer/id #uuid "f68c810b-465d-52e2-9a5e-049c583ea1a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f68c810b-465d-52e2-9a5e-049c583ea1a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407913} :answer-field/id #uuid "34f449e1-fb1a-5a4b-9b02-dc697805a6ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af73e85d-c908-5a06-97f4-04484193b6c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-81390" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b03f2ecd03a89d9484e7d28ab4cb34656b288c3a4554c9a4eeacbe9b61f692c.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}x\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and $y=2-x,$ above the line $y=1,$ as shown in the picture." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} \\\\ y=2-x & \\,⟹\\,x=2-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} & =2-y \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =(2-y)^{2} \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =4-4y+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-4y & =0 \\\\ y(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=1,$ the $y$-limits are \n$1\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/f8/f8fedf15e26f64fc344bd7db6ffaa659bd7c130f36f4a39389b6108136836104.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,2-y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\,dA={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,x\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{2}[\\,{x}^{2}\\,]_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{2}(4-{y}^{2}-(2-y)^{2})dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,(2y-{y}^{2})dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,(2y-{y}^{2})dy & ={[{y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(4-\\frac{8}{3})-(1-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f7718f-e014-5232-acb1-6773ab79f61d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b861f489-607a-54e8-8be4-cd68a12b910f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382596} {:answer/id #uuid "37b0d41c-cbf1-5780-8fe1-3886e27f1e49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382597} {:answer/id #uuid "23e016f9-01a7-5cfe-a9e8-d9d18a841e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382598} {:answer/id #uuid "95653e8c-81ec-5a74-9311-122bc157256b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382599} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c0e138-39c2-5eda-a4e4-d22f74e3ae99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23e016f9-01a7-5cfe-a9e8-d9d18a841e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382598} :answer-field/id #uuid "95f530d4-be17-5a5f-8b00-55f168260743" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f13b8b49-8217-594e-a1e7-695fd01b0cfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-89550" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d6f00e63a577dff29021e50add69cf916b3f74ed90530b5f4c9f46295950b416.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}{x}^{3}\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ above the $x$-axis, as shown in the picture." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}} & =\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} \\\\ 1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =4-{y}^{2} \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =16-4{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{y}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the $x$-axis, the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/ec/ec8853d64b58c40935b769745d5adb9ebc5c84b7f9407cc93dff4b809ed64ad5.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{x}^{3}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/4}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,{x}^{3}\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/4}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,{x}^{3}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/4}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,{x}^{3}\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,{[\\,\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}\\,]}_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/4}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{4}[{(4-{y}^{2})}^{2}-{(1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4})}^{2}]dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,[(16-8{y}^{2}+{y}^{4})-(1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{16})]dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,(15-\\frac{15}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{15}{16}{y}^{4})dy \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,(1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{16})dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15}{4}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,(1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{16})dy & =\\frac{15}{4}{[y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{80}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}[(2-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{2}{5})-0] \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
